  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Landen Roupp gave them a clean five innings and the offense vanished anyway.",
    "what_this_means": "This is what fragile looks like: a starter who earned the win and a lineup that recorded two hits and seven strikeouts. The bullpen collapsed in the seventh. Harris and Sanmartin combined to give up six runs in one inning. But that was almost secondary. The real story was that Roupp's 1-ER line in 5.1 IP meant nothing when the bats couldn't produce a single run. Detroit never had to work hard. Neither did their starter. The Giants made it easy. When the pitching is good enough to win and the offense refuses to show, the result looks exactly like this: a shutout at home in August. The margin between contention and drift is simple. It's what you do when the starter holds the door open.",
    "what_to_watch": "Troy Melton takes the mound for Detroit tonight with a 1.58 ERA that has no reason to fear what the Giants' lineup offered yesterday. Logan Webb's 3.35 ERA over his last four starts is the only opening they have.",
